    @* #488 · THE SCUTTLING PANEL. Owner: "we could have a self-destruct option in the ship, like reactor
       overload … in cases where we want to be sure." The third road: it pays NOTHING, which is the only
       reason it can mean anything. No confirmation dialog — the panel states her worth first, and the keys
       are the confirmation. *@
    @* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       #997 wave 4 · THE CARDS THAT BORROWED THE FAMILY'S FOOT — and this is the first of the thirteen.

       Wave 3 put every card ROOTED on `.view-object` on the shell and then wrote down what was left: thirteen
       more cards wearing a `<button class="view-object-close">` on a root of their OWN — the vent boards, the
       operating log, both treasure maps, the lift's car panel, the bar table and the satchel. They took the
       family's button and the family's wording without taking its root, so they were hand-rolling the same
       three lines the other ten had been.

       THEY KEEP THEIR ROOTS. A vent board is a vent board and not a view-object: the shell is the MECHANISM
       and the root class is the IDENTITY, and #995's completeness guard reads that name off this markup as
       typed, so it has to stay a lowercase class attribute exactly where it was. What changes is who draws
       the way out. Same three parameters as wave 3 — `Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare"` (the dismiss stays a DIRECT
       child, which is what `.treasure-map-card > .view-object-close` and every other #735 pin is written
       against), `StopClicks="true"` (the stopPropagation that was typed on the div), and
       `Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"` wired to the page's own close verb, the same one the backdrop behind
       it already calls.

       NOT ONE WORD MOVED, again: "Step away", "Not yet", "Log it", "Back to the board", "Close" and the
       wallet's own keep-it line are the exact strings their own buttons carried, and the two that carried a
       `title` still carry it. Nothing here is standing anywhere either, so wave 2's stranding rule is not
       needed on any of them — every one is a board, a panel, a map or a bag, and putting it down is already
       what its button did.

       THE ONE THAT DID NOT COME. #603's locked door keeps its hand-rolled button, and the guard names it
       with the reason: its way out is one of TWO buttons standing side by side in `.locked-door-actions`,
       beside the brighter "Check your items" the door exists to advertise. Bare draws the dismiss as the
       card's LAST DIRECT CHILD — which is the whole point of the frame — so shelling it would lift that
       button out of the row and drop it on its own line. That is a control moving on the screen. *@
    @if (_showScuttlePanel && _wreck is { } scWreck)
    {
        <ScuttlePanel _scuttleSecondsLeft="@_scuttleSecondsLeft" AbortTheOverload="@AbortTheOverload"
                      ArmTheOverload="@ArmTheOverload" CloseScuttlePanel="@CloseScuttlePanel" Dismiss="@Dismiss"
                      ScuttleMethods="@ScuttleMethods" scWreck="@scWreck" />
    }

