    @* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       #1021 · THE GALLEY, AS A CARD.

       Owner, of the full-screen Galley desk, verbatim: "We want to keep the news feed... refactor it so it
       can be elsewhere also, but this UI MUST GO!... This was our first version, it has no gen AI or
       visibility to the bar surroundings. So keep the features but I want it done in pop-up style like the
       work the case is."

       "LIKE THE WORK THE CASE IS" IS A SPECIFICATION, and it is the card twenty lines above this one: a
       click-to-close backdrop, StopClicks so the card does not shut under the captain's own hand, an
       OverlayFrame.Bare shell whose way out is its last direct child, and the room STILL THERE behind it.
       That last clause is the owner's second complaint answered without drawing anything: opened at the
       deck's CANTINA console the card hangs over the cantina's own art, which is the "visibility to the bar
       surroundings" the blacked-out desk never had. No new picture; the room was always there, the desk was
       just standing in front of it.

       THE FEATURES, VERBATIM AND IN ORDER. Nothing on this card is new prose — the rum locker's heading,
       its tot count, its two-state steady-legs line, "Pour a tot" and its tooltip, and the wire's own words
       are all lifted from Stations/Galley.razor, which this replaces. The pour is the SAME PourRumFromGalley
       the desk called, which is the same PourRum the deck's own locker calls: one rum ledger, and now three
       doors onto it that cannot disagree about the tot count.

       THE LOCKER LEADS AND THE WIRE SCROLLS UNDER IT. On a desk the two sat side by side because there was
       a screen's width to fill; on a card there is not, so the order is the one a captain wants — the thing
       you PRESS is above the thing you READ, and the wire's list is the only part that gives (its
       CappedScrollPanel lives inside NewsWirePanel). The card's own cap is 92vh, the satchel's, so the page
       itself never scrolls. *@
    @if (_galleyCardOpen)
    {
        <GalleyCard _lastRumLine="@_lastRumLine" _rumTots="@_rumTots" _tenderLine="@_tenderLine"
                    CloseGalleyCard="@CloseGalleyCard" Dismiss="@Dismiss"
                    GalleyFeedAmbientDays="@GalleyFeedAmbientDays" GalleyFeedItemCount="@GalleyFeedItemCount"
                    NewsFeed="@((int ambientCount) => NewsFeed(ambientCount))" PourRumFromGalley="@PourRumFromGalley" RumWobbleActive="@RumWobbleActive"
                    SimTime="@SimTime" />
    }

    @* #949 · THE PLOTTING CARD — the help the owner asked for, where the question gets asked.

       Owner, screenshot in hand: "We should have a help page where we show multi-step plan and the use of
       the schrub and burn. New player seeing this image would understand how to play."

       WHY A CARD WHEN /help/nav ALREADY EXISTS, in one line: the page is the read you do before you start,
       and the ? is pressed mid-plan with the panel open. A second tab answers that question by taking the
       question off the screen. The reasoning in full, and the whole door chain, is in Map.NavHelp.cs.

       It is the galley's shape exactly — .view-object over a click-to-close backdrop, one way out, Escape —
       so it inherits the family's cap, its inner scroll and its pinned foot (#735) with no new geometry.
       The CappedScrollPanel inside is what keeps the TITLE still while the six steps move under it: on a
       phone this card is taller than the glass, and a card whose heading scrolls away is a card you lose
       your place in. The words are Components/PlottingHelp.razor, which reads every button face it prints
       off the same Core call the panel draws it with. *@
    @if (_navHelpOpen)
    {
        <NavHelpCard CloseNavHelp="@CloseNavHelp" Dismiss="@Dismiss" />
    }

    @* #836 · THE FLETCH WALLET, FANNED WHILE HE CROSSES THE FLOOR. Owner: "I think I should be able to pick
       the badge I show the guard... like Fletch ... suppose we have 4 different ID's ... one of them real".

       It opens on the HAIL and it is gone by the time his hand is out — the 2026-08-08 ruling stands, the
       read is still automatic and still told on a card, and the only thing that moved is when the captain
       decided. One row per paper, and every row is the captain's own knowledge and nothing else: the glyph,
       the NAME ON IT, what it CLAIMS (its printed face, verbatim from the file that prints it) and what the
       book itself filed about that paper at this site. No percentages, no ticks, no oracle — the man has not
       read it yet, and neither has this dialog. *@
    @if (WalletFanIsUp && _surface is { } fanEx && TheWalletFan is { Count: > 1 } fanPapers)
    {
        <WalletFanCard ChooseThePaper="@ChooseThePaper" CloseTheWalletFan="@CloseTheWalletFan" Dismiss="@Dismiss"
                       NameOnYourOwnPapers="@NameOnYourOwnPapers" TheBookOn="@TheBookOn"
                       ThePaperInYourHandIs="@ThePaperInYourHandIs" fanEx="@fanEx" fanPapers="@fanPapers" />
    }

    @if (_viewObject is { } vo)
    {
        <ViewObjectCard CloseViewObject="@CloseViewObject" Dismiss="@Dismiss" KeepTheFind="@KeepTheFind"
                        LeaveTheFind="@LeaveTheFind" PressTheHit="@PressTheHit"
                        TheFindIsWaitingOnAnAnswer="@TheFindIsWaitingOnAnAnswer" TheKitsCardIsUp="@TheKitsCardIsUp"
                        TheKitSweeps="@TheKitSweeps" vo="@vo" />
    }

    @* THE CAPTAIN'S SELFIE (#400): the framed shot — the vista backdrop with the captain's #368 portrait
       disc composited into the corner (the "no digital trickery, the captain was HERE" homage), the
       boastful house-voice caption, and the note it's filed in the legend ledger. Click away / Close / E
       again dismisses it. Both the img and the portrait fall back cleanly (onerror-hide) if unpainted. *@
    @if (_selfieShot is { } shot)
    {
        <SelfieShotCard CloseSelfieShot="@CloseSelfieShot" Dismiss="@Dismiss" shot="@shot" />
    }

    @* The peak-moment nudge (#400 §3): after a big beat, "grab a selfie for the record?" — one-time, small,
       non-blocking. Take it (captures the themed shot) or wave it off for the session. *@
    @if (_selfieOffer is { } selfieOffer)
    {
        <SelfieOfferCard _selfieOffer="@_selfieOffer" AcceptSelfieOffer="@AcceptSelfieOffer"
                         DeclineSelfieOffer="@DeclineSelfieOffer" Dismiss="@Dismiss" selfieOffer="@selfieOffer" />
    }

    @* Hatch keypad: brought up by knocking on the specific locked hatch a crack job sent you to. Key in
       the code the Fixer quoted; right code pops the hatch (job → PickedUp), wrong code buzzes red. *@
    @if (_pinJob is { } pinJob)
    {
        <PinJobCard _pinHatch="@_pinHatch" _pinOutcome="@_pinOutcome" CancelPin="@CancelPin" Dismiss="@Dismiss"
                    PinClear="@PinClear" PinDisplay="@PinDisplay" PinPush="@PinPush" SubmitPin="@SubmitPin" />
    }

    @* Deck pulse toast: the Nav HUD that normally hosts the pulse line is hidden while
       you're walking the ship, so console quips (the HEAD 🚽 gag, cargo tally, vent report, shuttle
       standby) would render nowhere. Surface them over the canvas here, centred near the top. *@
    @if (_deckMode && _pulse.Message is not null)
    {
        <div class="deck-pulse-toast">@_pulse.Message</div>
    }

    @* A bar stranger's contract, slid across the table (go-ashore quests, M-Q1): shown over the deck
       canvas while ashore, with Take / Pass. Same overlay family as the deck-pulse-toast. *@
    @if (_deckMode && _pendingOffer is { } offer)
    {
        <PendingOfferCard _pendingOffer="@_pendingOffer" AcceptOffer="@AcceptOffer"
                          ContactDrinkOffer="@ContactDrinkOffer" CurrentKeep="@CurrentKeep"
                          DeclineOffer="@DeclineOffer" Dismiss="@Dismiss" GiverDisplay="@GiverDisplay"
